$DIR/main.p:17:5: cannot start square with go: it holds a function literal that captures out (main.p:16), whose closure record lives in the starting frame (spec section 4.7)
    A function literal that captures variables, and a method value such as
    c.handle, keep their closure record (the code to call, with the captured
    variables or a copy of the receiver) in the frame of the function that makes
    them. A func value points to that record. A go statement copies its function
    and arguments, but copying a func value copies only the pointer, so the
    puroutine may call through it after the frame is gone.
    The compiler reports this wherever it can tell: a capturing literal or a
    method value passed to go, or a local variable assigned only once, from one
    of them. Elsewhere the go statement checks when it runs, and panics.
    Library functions that start a puroutine with a func argument, such as
    sync.WaitGroup.Go and time.AfterFunc, follow the same rule and the compiler
    reports them the same way.
    To let the puroutine use the function's variables, start a function literal
    with a sync.Scope declared in the function instead: s.Task(func() {...})
    waits for it before s's block ends.
    Go moves captured variables and the receivers of method values to the heap.
    Plutonium never allocates behind the program's back, so the record stays in
    its frame (spec section 4.5).
    Pass the values the function needs, and start a function that takes them:
        go func(n int, out chan int) {
            out <- n * n
        }(n, out)
    (see "go starts a puroutine" in the Plutonium spec, section 4.7)
    (run "pluto explain E2594" for a longer explanation)

